Package: OptiSparseMET
Type: Package
Title: Sparse Multi-Environment Trial Design with Flexible Local Field Layout
Version: 0.2.0
Authors@R:
    person(
      given   = "Félicien",
      family  = "Akohoue",
      email   = "akohoue.f@gmail.com",
      role    = c("aut", "cre"),
      comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-2160-0182")
    )
Description: Constructs statistically connected and operationally feasible
    sparse multi-environment trial (MET) designs by integrating
    across-environment treatment allocation with within-environment field
    layout. Random-balanced allocation prioritises complete treatment coverage,
    whereas equireplicate allocation enforces equal network replication when
    the requested degree sequence is realisable (after Montesinos-Lopez et al.
    2023 <doi:10.1002/tpg2.20305>). Common treatments provide direct
    cross-environment connectivity; robust optimisation can determine their
    number, identities, and site-specific replication under uncertainty,
    capacity limits, and a single network-wide seed inventory. Genetic
    structure can be represented by family labels or additive genomic and
    pedigree relationship matrices. Dominance relationships are optional and
    intended for hybrid applications. Local layouts include augmented,
    partially replicated, randomised complete-block-type, and alpha
    row-column designs. Mixed-model evaluation uses A-optimality,
    D-optimality, and mean coefficient of determination (CDmean) criteria for
    genomic selection (Rincent et al. 2012
    <doi:10.1534/genetics.112.141473>). Coupled information-matrix and
    genotype-by-environment simulation tools quantify prediction error,
    reliability, selection accuracy, genetic gain, and Monte Carlo uncertainty
    from delivered plot counts. A paired benchmarking framework compares
    complete, random, M3, M4, and optimised designs across covariance,
    variance-component, site-loss, plot, cost, and seed scenarios. It also
    quantifies known-truth covariance recovery, environmental missingness
    sensitivity, and decision stability. Quality-controlled weather, soil, management,
    and geographic data are represented by separate main-effect and
    cross-modality interaction kernels. Pre-specified variable interactions
    can be represented by dedicated residualised tensor kernels with
    strong-heredity parent structures and complete construction audits.
    Historical MET responses, when available, calibrate environmental kernels
    by blocked validation and estimate genetic environment covariance by
    restricted maximum likelihood with diagonal, factor-analytic, or
    unstructured models that retain missing genotype-environment cells.
    Component-wise interaction assessment uses blocked prediction,
    multiplicity control, and bootstrap inclusion stability.
    Without historical responses, the central covariance is the identity
    matrix and unweighted kernels define separate maximin sensitivity
    scenarios. Stability-screened clustering infers descriptive
    mega-environments and returns an unpartitioned network when grouping is
    unsupported. Target-population weights, environment-specific residual
    variances and plot costs, integer partial replication, and full local
    treatment-information matrices feed the coupled design objective. A
    matrix-free preconditioned-conjugate-gradient solver supports larger
    networks. The end-to-end pipeline joins capacity analysis, common-set
    selection, allocation, seed accounting, local field design, robust
    evaluation, and unified field-book assembly; versioned design objects
    reconcile the released allocation, replication, fieldbooks, diagnostics,
    and provenance.
